  DEFECT 1 (attempt 1) -- lifetime tax nominal while the caption claimed
  today's dollars -- INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMED FIXED:

  (a) The deflation matches the Tax Optimizer's LifetimeTaxReal pattern.
      Read side by side:
        tax_optimizer.go:236-241
          for _, ys := range proj.YearlySummaries {
              deflator := ys.CumulativeInflation
              if deflator <= 0 { deflator = 1 }
              cand.LifetimeTaxReal += ys.Taxes / deflator
        handlers_sweep.go:48-58 (lifetimeRealTaxAndIRMAA)
          for _, ys := range proj.YearlySummaries {
              deflator := ys.CumulativeInflation
              if deflator <= 0 { deflator = 1 }
              tax   += ys.Taxes / deflator
              irmaa += ys.IRMAA / deflator
      Same field (ys.Taxes = federal+state+NIIT), same deflator, same
      `<= 0 -> 1` guard for pre-projection/unset years. IRMAA gets the same
      treatment, which is what makes the caption's "all in today's dollars"
      true for both columns rather than only one.

  (b) The fixture genuinely makes real and nominal diverge -- measured, not
      assumed. Overlay probe printing both figures on sweepScenarioSettings():
      $ go test -count=1 -overlay <scratchpad>/overlay.json \
          -run TestZZCheckerProbeRealVsNominal -v ./internal/handlers/whatif/
        amt=0      real=55928.36 nominal=95712.48 ratio=0.584  survives=true
        amt=50000  real=45722.73 nominal=58836.03 ratio=0.777  survives=true
        amt=100000 real=76482.92 nominal=85409.17 ratio=0.895  survives=false
      The test's asserted row (amount=100000) diverges by $8,926.25 -- ~10.5%,
      far outside its 0.01 tolerance. The test also refuses to pass vacuously:
      lines 394-396 t.Fatalf "fixture does not exercise the defect" unless
      real < nominal strictly, so a future fixture edit that collapses the two
      figures fails loudly instead of silently rubber-stamping.

  (c) MUTATION TEST -- the new test actually catches the original defect.
      I reverted the production line to the nominal path via `go test -overlay`
      (scratchpad copy of handlers_sweep.go with
      `row.LifetimeTax = analysis.BuildTax(proj, in).TotalTaxPaid`; the repo
      file was never touched) and re-ran the full sweep suite:
      $ go test -count=1 -overlay <scratchpad>/overlay2.json \
          -run 'ConversionSweep|Sweep' -v ./internal/handlers/whatif/
        --- FAIL: TestHandleWhatIfConversionSweep_LifetimeTaxIsRealNotNominal
            LifetimeTax = 85409.16630406468, want 76482.9158239755
              (per-year deflated sum)
            LifetimeTax = 85409.16630406468 equals nominal TotalTaxPaid
              85409.16630406468 -- must report real, not nominal
        (all 10 other sweep tests still PASS)
      So the new test is exactly and solely load-bearing for DEFECT 1: it is
      the only test in the suite that fails when the defect is reintroduced,
      and it fails on both its assertions. This also independently confirms
      the worker's manifest line 14-17 regression claim, including its exact
      numbers (85409.17 nominal / 76482.92 real).

  Caption now matches the code (conversion-sweep.html:76-81): "Ending balance,
  lifetime tax, and lifetime IRMAA are all in today's dollars
  (inflation-deflated), summed across projection years." Attempt 1's caption
  said IRMAA was nominal and tax was real, and neither was true.

  NOTE for the lead, not a defect: the spec's Behavior section (line 37) says
  "lifetime tax: reuse `analysis.BuildTax(proj, in)` totals". The fix
  deliberately departs from that sentence, because BuildTax.TotalTaxPaid is
  nominal and was the cause of DEFECT 1. No numbered acceptance criterion
  (1-7) mentions BuildTax, so this does not fail any criterion -- but the
  Behavior text is now stale against the code and SPEC/CONVERSION_SWEEP_SPEC
  should be amended if the lead wants the document to stay accurate.

